## Test Runner (`tests/test_runner.c`)

Use a minimal hand-rolled test runner — no external frameworks:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

/* Declared by each test file. */
int test_args(void);
int test_output(void);

int main(void)
{
    int failed = 0;

    failed += test_args();
    failed += test_output();

    if (failed == 0) {
        printf("All tests passed.\n");
        return EXIT_SUCCESS;
    }

    fprintf(stderr, "%d test(s) failed.\n", failed);
    return EXIT_FAILURE;
}
```

Each `test_*` function returns the number of failed assertions (0 = all passed).

---

## Helper Macro

Define a lightweight assertion macro in `tests/test_runner.c` or a shared `tests/test_utils.h`:

```c
#define ASSERT_EQ_STR(got, expected, label)                        \
    do {                                                           \
        if (strcmp((got), (expected)) != 0) {                      \
            fprintf(stderr, "FAIL [%s]: expected \"%s\", got \"%s\"\n", \
                    (label), (expected), (got));                   \
            return 1;                                              \
        }                                                          \
        printf("PASS [%s]\n", (label));                            \
    } while (0)

#define ASSERT_EQ_INT(got, expected, label)                        \
    do {                                                           \
        if ((got) != (expected)) {                                 \
            fprintf(stderr, "FAIL [%s]: expected %d, got %d\n",   \
                    (label), (expected), (got));                   \
            return 1;                                              \
        }                                                          \
        printf("PASS [%s]\n", (label));                            \
    } while (0)
```

