## Required Test Cases

### Unit: `args_parse` — default output

```c
/* tests/test_args.c */
#include "args.h"
#include "test_utils.h"
#include <string.h>

int test_args(void)
{
    Args a;
    char *argv[] = { "yes-toto", NULL };

    ASSERT_EQ_INT(args_parse(1, argv, &a), 0, "args_parse: no args returns 0");
    ASSERT_EQ_STR(a.output, "y", "args_parse: default output is y");
    ASSERT_EQ_INT(a.show_help, 0, "args_parse: show_help is 0");
    ASSERT_EQ_INT(a.show_version, 0, "args_parse: show_version is 0");
    return 0;
}
```

### Unit: `args_parse` — custom string

```c
char *argv2[] = { "yes-toto", "hello", NULL };
ASSERT_EQ_INT(args_parse(2, argv2, &a), 0, "args_parse: custom arg returns 0");
ASSERT_EQ_STR(a.output, "hello", "args_parse: output is hello");
```

### Unit: `args_parse` — multiple args joined

```c
char *argv3[] = { "yes-toto", "a", "b", "c", NULL };
ASSERT_EQ_INT(args_parse(4, argv3, &a), 0, "args_parse: multi args returns 0");
ASSERT_EQ_STR(a.output, "a b c", "args_parse: joined output is a b c");
```

### Unit: `args_parse` — `--help` flag

```c
char *argv4[] = { "yes-toto", "--help", NULL };
ASSERT_EQ_INT(args_parse(2, argv4, &a), 0, "args_parse: --help returns 0");
ASSERT_EQ_INT(a.show_help, 1, "args_parse: show_help is 1");
```

### Unit: `args_parse` — `--version` flag

```c
char *argv5[] = { "yes-toto", "--version", NULL };
ASSERT_EQ_INT(args_parse(2, argv5, &a), 0, "args_parse: --version returns 0");
ASSERT_EQ_INT(a.show_version, 1, "args_parse: show_version is 1");
```

### Unit: `args_parse` — unknown flag

```c
char *argv6[] = { "yes-toto", "--foo", NULL };
ASSERT_EQ_INT(args_parse(2, argv6, &a), -1, "args_parse: unknown flag returns -1");
```

### Integration: default output reads N lines

```c
/* tests/test_output.c */
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "test_utils.h"

#define BINARY "./yes-toto"
#define LINES  5

int test_output(void)
{
    FILE *pipe = popen(BINARY, "r");
    if (!pipe) {
        fprintf(stderr, "FAIL: could not spawn %s\n", BINARY);
        return 1;
    }

    char line[256];
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < LINES; i++) {
        if (!fgets(line, sizeof(line), pipe)) {
            fprintf(stderr, "FAIL: unexpected EOF at line %d\n", i);
            pclose(pipe);
            return 1;
        }
        /* Strip trailing newline. */
        line[strcspn(line, "\n")] = '\0';
        ASSERT_EQ_STR(line, "y", "output_loop: default line");
    }

    pclose(pipe); /* Closing pipe sends SIGPIPE — yes-toto must exit cleanly. */
    return 0;
}
```

### Integration: custom string

```c
FILE *pipe2 = popen(BINARY " hello", "r");
/* read 3 lines, assert each equals "hello" */
```

### Integration: multiple args joined

```c
FILE *pipe3 = popen(BINARY " a b c", "r");
/* read 3 lines, assert each equals "a b c" */
```

---

## Key Rules for Tests

- **Always use `pclose()`** after reading — this closes the pipe, which sends SIGPIPE to `yes-toto` and causes it to exit cleanly. Never leave zombie processes.
- **Never hardcode an absolute binary path** — always use the relative path `"./yes-toto"` which is valid after `make`.
- **Never use `sleep()`** in tests — pipe closure is sufficient to stop the child.
- **Test binary must not link `src/main.o`** — exclude it in the Makefile to avoid a duplicate `main` linker error.
- **Each test function returns an int** — 0 for all passed, positive integer for the number of failures.
- Run the full suite with: `make test`

